WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:08.133
Episode 206 was the start of a very

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.302
interesting emotional 
arc for me.

00:00:10.302 --> 00:00:13.430
I think that often times,
African American women

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.432
in corporate environments 
are tokenized

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:17.226
and they are taken
advantage of, because

00:00:17.226 --> 00:00:19.853
they need help to
get up the ladder.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.231
I can see you’re
obviously hurting.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.400
Can you see that, Chip?

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.445
I think Karen Pittman portrays
her with such magnificence

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:31.740
that you can see the pain,
you can see the complexity,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.075
and you can see her struggle.

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.369
I trust you will
do the right thing,

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:37.788
that you won’t let your
personal involvement

00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:38.956
cloud your judgement?

00:00:38.956 --> 00:00:42.209
During this time
there was an undeniable

00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:46.004
new anti-Asian sentiment
that was happening.

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:48.799
It was uncomfortable
to talk about, but

00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:51.843
I was grateful that we
were able to incorporate

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:56.431
it and talk about it with
a level of care and dexterity

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:59.101
in terms of how we
were gonna present this.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.312
Can you get me pictures or not?

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:03.605
One of the most
interesting things

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:05.691
that the show attempts to do is

00:01:05.691 --> 00:01:08.568
wrestle with the idea
of just how hard it is

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.070
to be an adult in a world where

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.406
the foundations are
shifting all the time.

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:15.993
I love Cory’s moral ambiguity,

00:01:15.993 --> 00:01:19.663
he does some of the most
incredible, selfless things.

00:01:19.663 --> 00:01:22.040
He does some of the
most horrible things

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.584
and he does them
with equal enthusiasm.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.962
He’s a strategist, and I think

00:01:26.962 --> 00:01:29.464
when his strategy doesn’t
work he pivots quickly.

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:32.384
Someone being outed,
against their will.

00:01:32.384 --> 00:01:34.386
This is not about being outed.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:36.555
When Laura leaves
Bradley’s dressing room,

00:01:36.555 --> 00:01:39.349
she turns around
and says to her,

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:41.351
“And you’re right,
it’s wrong what happened"

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:43.979
The first time I said
the line I thought

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:45.856
I’m gonna cry and 
I don’t wanna cry here

00:01:45.856 --> 00:01:47.524
it’s not my scene, 
this is her scene

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.694
but I realized when I actually
verbalized those lines

00:01:50.694 --> 00:01:52.904
it’s just the worst feeling
you could ever have.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.490
Mom’s been calling,
she’s embarrassed!

00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:57.200
I think there are
a lot of people

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.578
who are very constrained
by their upbringing.

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:02.497
I see, Bradley growing
in this moment

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:05.751
and slowly pulling herself away
from a family that tried to

00:02:05.751 --> 00:02:08.086
define her with 
very narrow terms.

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:10.213
I have a lot of 
clarity right now.

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:13.383
In this season, there’s 
a lot of self-examination.

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.135
Who are they?
What matters?

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.971
And we question that a lot
through our characters.

